<Docs slug="harnesses">
  <h1>Harnesses</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Claude Code, Codex, Pi, and OpenCode get live status indicators and learn the <code>ouijit</code> CLI
    automatically. No configuration required.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <p>Run <code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>pi</code>, or <code>opencode</code> in any Ouijit terminal and two things happen:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Live status</strong>. The terminal card shows whether the agent is thinking or idle, visible
      across the whole stack. You can tell which sessions need attention without bringing each one forward.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CLI awareness</strong>. The <a href="/docs/cli/"><code>ouijit</code> CLI</a> reference is appended
      to the agent's system prompt, so the agent can create tasks, advance the board, stage review comments, and
      open panels from the shell. See <a href="/docs/agents/">Agents</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    Ouijit installs a thin wrapper for each supported harness into the terminal's <code>PATH</code>. The wrapper
    shadows the real binary, injects the CLI reference into the harness's system prompt, and wires status hooks
    back to Ouijit. The mechanism differs per harness:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Harness</th><th>Status wiring</th><th>System prompt</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Claude Code</strong></td>
        <td><code>--settings</code> with <code>UserPromptSubmit</code> / <code>Stop</code> hooks</td>
        <td><code>--append-system-prompt-file</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Codex</strong></td>
        <td><code>-c hooks.&lt;Event&gt;=...</code> overrides, pre-trusted so they fire without <code>/hooks</code> approval</td>
        <td><code>-c developer_instructions=...</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Pi</strong></td>
        <td><code>--extension</code> loading a generated TypeScript extension that subscribes to <code>turn_start</code> / <code>turn_end</code></td>
        <td><code>--append-system-prompt</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>OpenCode</strong></td>
        <td>Status plugin loaded from opencode's auto-load dir, reporting off the <code>session.status</code> event</td>
        <td>CLI reference injected via <code>OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT</code> (opencode has no system-prompt CLI flag)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    When <code>OUIJIT_API_URL</code> is unset, meaning the harness runs outside an Ouijit terminal, the wrappers
    exec the real binary unmodified.
  </p>

  <h2>Sandboxed terminals</h2>
  <p>Status indicators work inside both <a href="/docs/sandbox/">sandbox backends</a>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lima VM</strong>. Claude settings, Codex config, the Pi extension, and the opencode plugin are written into the VM's home directory, and their hooks reach back over <code>host.lima.internal</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>nono</strong>. Terminals run on the host, so the agent uses your real config and hooks reach the hook server over localhost.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Notifications</h2>
  <p>
    When an agent finishes a turn, Ouijit plays a sound. If the window isn't focused, you also get an OS
    notification. Turn the sound off under <strong>App Settings &rarr; Sound</strong>.
  </p>
</Docs>
